 L166:   * cite83†Apache-2.0 license L167: \nL168: More items\nL169: \nL170: cite84†Image: Xiaomi-MiMo L171: \nL172: ### ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━\nL173: MiMo Audio: Audio Language Models are Few-Shot Learners\nL174: ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━\nL175: \nL176: | cite85†🤗 HuggingFace†huggingface.co | cite77†📄 Paper | cite86†📰 Blog†xiaomimimo.github.io | cite87†📊 MiMo-Audio-Eval |\nL177: ## Introduction\nL178: Existing audio language models typically rely on task-specific fine-tuning to accomplish particular audio tasks. In contrast, humans are able to generalize to new audio tasks with only a few examples or simple instructions. GPT-3 has shown that scaling next-token prediction pretraining enables strong generalization capabilities in text, and we believe this paradigm is equally applicable to the audio domain.\nL179: By scaling MiMo-Audio's pretraining data to over one hundred million of hours, we observe the emergence of few-shot learning capabilities across a diverse set of audio tasks. We develop a systematic evaluation of these capabilities and find that MiMo-Audio-7B-Base achieves SOTA performance on both speech intelligence and audio understanding benchmarks among open-source models.\nL180: Beyond standard metrics, MiMo-Audio-7B-Base generalizes to tasks absent from its training data, such as voice conversion, style transfer, and speech editing. MiMo-Audio-7B-Base also demonstrates powerful speech continuation capabilities, capable of generating highly realistic talk shows, recitations, livestreaming and debates. At the post-training stage, we curate a diverse instruction-tuning corpus and introduce thinking mechanisms into both audio understanding and generation.\nL181: MiMo-Audio-7B-Instruct achieves open-source SOTA on audio understanding benchmarks, spoken dialogue benchmarks and instruct-TTS evaluations, approaching or surpassing closed-source models.\nL182: ## Architecture\nL183: ### MiMo-Audio-Tokenizer\nL184: \nL185: MiMo-Audio-Tokenizer is a 1.2B-parameter Transformer operating at 25 Hz. It employs an eight-layer RVQ stack to generate 200 tokens per second. By jointly optimizing semantic and reconstruction objectives, we train MiMo-Audio-Tokenizer from scratch on a 10-million-hour corpus, achieving superior reconstruction quality and facilitating downstream language modeling.\nL186: MiMo-Audio couples a patch encoder, an LLM, and a patch decoder to improve modeling efficiency for high-rate sequences and bridge the length mismatch between speech and text. The patch encoder aggregates four consecutive time steps of RVQ tokens into a single patch, downsampling the sequence to a 6.25 Hz representation for the LLM. The patch decoder autoregressively generates the full 25 Hz RVQ token sequence via a delayed-generation scheme.\nL187: ### MiMo-Audio\nL188: \nL189: ## Explore MiMo-Audio Now! 🚀🚀🚀\nL190: \nL191:   * 🎧 Try the Hugging Face demo: cite88†MiMo-Audio Demo†huggingface.co L192:   * 📰 Read the Official Blog: cite86†MiMo-Audio Blog†xiaomimimo.github.io L193:   * 📄 Dive into the Technical Report: cite77†MiMo-Audio Technical Report L194: ## Model Download\nL195: Models  | 🤗 Hugging Face\nL196: --- | ---\nL197: MiMo-Audio-Tokenizer  | cite89†XiaomiMiMo/MiMo-Audio-Tokenizer†huggingface.co L198: MiMo-Audio-7B-Base  | cite90†XiaomiMiMo/MiMo-Audio-7B-Base†huggingface.co L199: MiMo-Audio-7B-Instruct  | cite91†XiaomiMiMo/MiMo-Audio-7B-Instruct†huggingface.co L200: \nL201:     pip install huggingface-hub\nL202: \nL203:     hf download XiaomiMiMo/MiMo-Audio-Tokenizer --local-dir ./models/MiMo-Audio-Tokenizer\nL204:     hf download XiaomiMiMo/MiMo-Audio-7B-Base --local-dir ./models/MiMo-Audio-7B-Base\nL205:     hf download XiaomiMiMo/MiMo-Audio-7B-Instruct --local-dir ./models/MiMo-Audio-7B-Instruct\nL206: ## Getting Started\nL207: \nL208: Spin up the MiMo-Audio demo in minutes with the built-in Gradio app.\nL209: \nL210: ### Prerequisites (Linux)\nL211: \nL212:   * Python 3.12\nL213:   * CUDA >= 12.0\nL214: ### Installation\nL215: \nL216:     git clone https://github.com/XiaomiMiMo/MiMo-Audio.git\nL217:     cd MiMo-Audio\nL218:     pip install -r requirements.txt\nL219:     pip install flash-attn==2.7.4.post1\nL220: \nL221: Note\nL222: \nL223: If the compilation of flash-attn takes too long, you can download the precompiled wheel and install it manually:\nL224: \nL225:   * cite92†Download Precompiled Wheel L226: \nL227:     pip install /path/to/flash_attn-2.7.4.post1+cu12torch2.6cxx11abiFALSE-cp312-cp312-linux_x86_64.whl\nL228: ### Run the demo\nL229: \nL230:     python run_mimo_audio.py\nL231: \nL232: This launches a local Gradio interface where you can try MiMo-Audio interactively.\nL233: \nL234: Enter the local paths for `MiMo-Audio-Tokenizer` and `MiMo-Audio-7B-Instruct`, then enjoy the full functionality of MiMo-Audio!\nL235: \nL236: ## Inference Scripts\nL237: \nL238: ### Base Model\nL239: \nL240: We provide an example script to explore the in-context learning capabilities of `MiMo-Audio-7B-Base`.\nL241: See: cite79†`inference_example_pretrain.py` L242: ### Instruct Model\nL243: \nL244: To try the instruction-tuned model `MiMo-Audio-7B-Instruct`, use the corresponding inference script.\nL245: See: cite80†`inference_example_sft.py` L246: \nL247: ## Evaluation Toolkit\nL248: \nL249: Full evaluation suite are available at 🌐cite87†MiMo-Audio-Eval .\nL250: \nL251: This toolkit is designed to evaluate MiMo-Audio and other recent audio LLMs as mentioned in the paper. It provides a flexible and extensible framework, supporting a wide range of datasets, tasks, and models.\nL252: ## Citation\nL253: \nL254:     @misc{coreteam2025mimoaudio,\nL255:           title={MiMo-Audio: Audio Language Models are Few-Shot Learners},\nL256:           author={LLM-Core-Team Xiaomi},\nL257:           year={2025},\nL258:           url={https://github.com/XiaomiMiMo/MiMo-Audio},\nL259:     }\nL260: \nL261: ## Contact\nL262: \nL263: Please contact us at mimo@xiaomi.com or open an issue if you have any questions.\nL264: \nL265: ## About\nL266: \nL267: MiMo-Audio: Audio Language Models are Few-Shot Learners\nL268: \nL269: cite93†xiaomimimo.github.io/MiMo-Audio-Demo/†xiaomimimo.github.io L270: ### Resources\nL271: \nL272: cite94†Readme L273: \nL274: cite95†Apache-2.0 license L275: \nL276: cite96†Activity L277: \nL278: cite97†Custom properties L279: \nL280: ### Stars\nL281: \nL282: 1.1k stars\nL283: \nL284: ### Watchers\nL285: \nL286: 17 watching\nL287: \nL288: ### Forks\nL289: \nL290: cite98†108 forks L291: \nL292: cite99†Report repository L293: \nL294: ## Releases\nL295: \nL296: ## Packages\nL297: \nL298: ## Contributors\nL299: \nL300: ## Languages\nL301: \nL302: You can’t perform that action at this time.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2509.09255v1] Sensible Agent: A Framework for Unobtrusive Interaction with Proactive AR Agents (https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09255v1)\nciteturn3373view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09255v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 161\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Human-Computer Interaction\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 11 Sep 2025 (this version), latest version 30 Oct 2025 (cite5†v2 )]\nL9: # Title:Sensible Agent: A Framework for Unobtrusive Interaction with Proactive AR Agents\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Geonsun Lee , cite7†Min Xia , cite8†Nels Numan , cite9†Xun Qian , cite10†David Li , cite11†Yanhe Chen , cite12†Achin Kulshrestha , cite13†Ishan Chatterjee , cite14†Yinda Zhang , cite15†Dinesh Manocha , cite16†David Kim , cite17†Ruofei Du L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Sensible Agent: A Framework for Unobtrusive Interaction with Proactive AR Agents, by Geonsun Lee and 11 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite18†View PDF cite19†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Proactive AR agents promise context-aware assistance, but their interactions often rely on explicit voice prompts or responses, which can be disruptive or socially awkward. We introduce Sensible Agent, a framework designed for unobtrusive interaction with these proactive agents. Sensible Agent dynamically adapts both \"what\" assistance to offer and, crucially, \"how\" to deliver it, based on real-time multimodal context sensing.\nL17: Informed by an expert workshop (n=12) and a data annotation study (n=40), the framework leverages egocentric cameras, multimodal sensing, and Large Multimodal Models (LMMs) to infer context and suggest appropriate actions delivered via minimally intrusive interaction modes. We demonstrate our prototype on an XR headset through a user study (n=10) in both AR and VR scenarios.\nL18: Results indicate that Sensible Agent significantly reduces perceived interaction effort compared to voice-prompted baseline, while maintaining high usability and achieving higher preference.\nL19: Subjects:  | Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite20†arXiv:2509.09255 [cs.HC]\nL21:    | (or cite21†arXiv:2509.09255v1 [cs.HC] for this version)\nL22:    | cite22†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.09255†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: Related DOI (Digital Object Identifier):  | cite23†https://doi.org/10.1145/3746059.3747748†doi.org DOI(s) linking to related resources\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Geonsun Lee [cite24†view email ]\nL27: [v1] Thu, 11 Sep 2025 08:40:26 UTC (26,825 KB)\nL28: cite5†[v2] Thu, 30 Oct 2025 19:39:46 UTC (26,825 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled Sensible Agent: A Framework 